  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Periods of rain today through Wednesday. \nGreatest areal coverage tonight and Tuesday morning. Widespread \nflooding unlikely, isolated minor flooding possible.\n\nConvection continues to slide south of the CWA with much\nstability overhead both due to the clouds I-80 southward, but\nalso because of the dry air infiltration from the N. Dewpoint \nat JST is in the m40s at 17Z. Dewpoints are creeping up across \nthe south-central and southeastern counties. But, just nearing \n60F now. Thus, it will make it easier for rain to get to the \nground there. But, they are not the most-risky locations for \nflooding. The Laurel Highlands, mainly our SW 4 counties, have \nthe lowest FFG/FFHs. But, much of the statistical guidance keeps\nrain intensities (QPF/1,3&6hr time frames) above those \nthresholds for flooding to occur. There is still a ~20% chc for \nrainfall intensities to exceed FFG tonight/Tues AM in the SW, \nbut we'd need to get some instability and deep convection to tap\nall the 1.5\" PWAT overhead. After consultation with LWX, we're \ngoing to continue to hold off on any flood watches. Expect some \nminor (ponding of water on roads/ditches, and small \ncreeks/streams) flooding due to the extended duration of the \nlight to moderate rainfall. Dry air continues to be shoved into \nthe area from the N through the next 48 hours. A tight gradient \nof precip may occur along or just north of I-80 this evening as \na slight enhancement to the rain is expected while a short wave \ntrough/MCV moves overhead W to E along I-80. There could be a \nltg strike/thunder rumble here or there, but it will be sparse.\n\nWe'll continue to evaluate the near term guidance for hints of\nheavier than currently-expected precip.\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Trending drier later in the week as a coastal \nstorm develops offshore.\n\nNortheast flow Wednesday into Thursday between 1038 mb high in\nQuebec and a developing coastal low will advect in dewpoints in\nthe 40s and perhaps even the 30s at the sfc Wed-Thu, with even\ndrier air in the 500-850 mb layer. This should bring an end to\nthe rainfall for at least a couple of days. The next feature to\nmonitor will be the coastal storm, which may meander off the New\nEngland and Mid Atlantic coast into the early part of next week\nwith this blocking high to its north. Such blocky patterns \nare notoriously difficult to predict with respect to sfc\nfeatures and precip potential/placement/timing. Depending on the\nsize of the coastal storm and how much it retrogrades, rain will\nbe possible in at least east central PA between Fri and early\nnext week, but confidence is low. Higher confidence in wind \ngusts of 15-25 mph and below normal temps through the end of the\nweek.",
